<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carraresi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1318–1405</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as da Carrara family</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Carraresi, or da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a>, were a family who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> as lords from 1318, with interruptions, until 1405. Patrons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> and of artists, they made Padua a cultural centre but were caught between Verona, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. Venice conquered Padua in 1405, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-novello-da-carrara">Francesco Novello da Carrara</a> and his sons were strangled in a Venetian prison in 1406.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carraresi.jpg" alt="Carraresi" width="320" height="350" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MacMoreno · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stemma_Nobiliare_Carrara.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
